tdeinit: defer to an already-running tdeinit instead of killing it.

init_tdeinit_socket(), when it finds the per-display socket already present and
connectable, used to TERMINATE the running tdeinit and take over ("newest
wins").  On substrate the bootstrap path is re-entered under load —
tdeinit_phase1, kwrapper and every TDEApplication can (re)start tdeinit — so
kill-and-takeover degenerated into an endless respawn loop: each new tdeinit
unlink()s + rebinds the socket, a client connecting in that window gets
ECONNREFUSED and bootstraps yet another tdeinit, which kills this one, ad
infinitum; the desktop never settled (twin/kicker/kdesktop never registered,
the panel never came up) and a core was pegged forever.

Defer instead: a connect() success means a live tdeinit already owns the
socket, so signal our bootstrap parent that we are "up" (start_tdeinit returns,
the caller proceeds to use the existing tdeinit) and exit cleanly without
disturbing its socket.  connect() only succeeds against a live listener, so a
genuinely stale socket still falls through to the unlink()+rebind path.

Verified: the upstream starttde respawn loop is gone (0 "Shutting down running
client" vs an infinite stream).  NOTE: this removes the loop but does not by
itself bring up the desktop — a separate, still-unsolved issue remains where
tdeinit's whole process group dies shortly after launching TDELauncher.
--- a/tdeinit/tdeinit.cpp
+++ b/tdeinit/tdeinit.cpp
@@ -881,12 +881,29 @@
 
      if(connect(s, (struct sockaddr *)&server, socklen) == 0)
      {
-        fprintf(stderr, "[tdeinit] Shutting down running client.\n");
-        tdelauncher_header request_header;
-        request_header.cmd = LAUNCHER_TERMINATE_TDEINIT;
-        request_header.arg_length = 0;
-        write(s, &request_header, sizeof(request_header));
-        sleep(1); // Give it some time
+        /* substrate: a healthy tdeinit is already listening here.  Upstream
+         * kills it and takes over ("newest wins"), but on substrate the
+         * bootstrap path gets re-entered under load — tdeinit_phase1, kwrapper
+         * and every TDEApplication can (re)start tdeinit — so kill-and-takeover
+         * becomes an endless respawn loop: each new tdeinit unlink()s + rebinds
+         * the socket, and any client connecting in that window gets ECONNREFUSED
+         * and bootstraps yet another tdeinit, which kills this one, and so on.
+         * The desktop never settles (twin/kicker/kdesktop never come up).
+         *
+         * Instead DEFER to the running tdeinit: signal our bootstrap parent that
+         * we are "up" (so start_tdeinit returns and the caller proceeds to use
+         * the existing tdeinit) and exit cleanly without disturbing its socket.
+         * connect() only succeeds against a live listener, so a genuinely stale
+         * socket still falls through to the unlink()+rebind path below. */
+        close(s);
+        if (d.initpipe[1] != -1)
+        {
+           char c = 0;
+           write(d.initpipe[1], &c, 1);
+           close(d.initpipe[1]);
+           d.initpipe[1] = -1;
+        }
+        exit(0);
      }
      close(s);
   }
